WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
A cryptographic hash maps input data to a fixed-size digest in a one-way manner.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:14.120
Hashes can help identify data, detect accidental or unauthorized change when used appropriately, and support larger protocols.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:20.940
They are different from ordinary checksums, which are usually designed for error detection rather than adversarial conditions.

4
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.280
They are also not encryption.

5
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:29.380
A hash does not provide confidentiality because it does not hide the original data in a reversible form.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:38.540
Message authentication codes, including HMAC concepts at a high level, combine a secret key with message data to help verify integrity and authenticity.

7
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.320
A plain hash may show that two inputs are the same, but it does not prove who created the digest.

8
00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.460
A MAC can help a receiver verify that someone with the shared secret produced the message.

9
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:57.240
This is useful for APIs, webhooks, internal messages, and integrity checks where both parties share a secret.

10
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.240
Password hashing is a separate category.

11
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:05.300
Passwords should not be encrypted for storage because the application should not be able to recover them.

12
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:12.280
Instead, it should store password verifiers created with password hashing approaches designed for slow, one-way verification.

13
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.140
Salts help ensure that identical passwords do not produce identical stored verifiers when used correctly.

14
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.960
Work factors and memory-hard concepts make large-scale guessing more expensive if hashes are exposed.

15
00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:33.240
Fast general-purpose hashes are poor choices for password storage because speed helps attackers test many guesses quickly after a database exposure.

16
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.740
Developers should not blur these tools together.

17
00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:44.960
Use hashes for the right integrity and identification purposes, MACs when authenticity with a shared secret is needed, and dedicated password hashing libraries for passwords.

18
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.140
Choosing the right tool matters more than recognizing the name of a primitive.
